<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta http-equiv="X-UA-Compatible" content="IE=edge">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Daily Challenge - Day 1</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>
    <header>
        <p>Daily HTML Challenge</p>
        <h1>Day 1: Explore More HTML Tags</h1>
    </header>
    <main>
        <section class="challenge">
            <h2>Today’s challenge</h2>
            <p>
                Welcome to Day 1! HTML has useful elements for more than headings
                and paragraphs. Today, explore tags that add meaning to text and
                make extra information available when readers need it.
            </p>
            <p>
                Start with <abbr title="HyperText Markup Language">HTML</abbr>,
                then look for the <mark>highlighted ideas</mark> as you explore.
            </p>
        </section>
        <section class="instructions">
            <h2>Tags to try</h2>
            <ol>
                <li>Use <code>&lt;abbr&gt;</code> to explain an abbreviation.</li>
                <li>Use <code>&lt;blockquote&gt;</code> for a longer quotation.</li>
                <li>Use <code>&lt;cite&gt;</code> to identify a creative work.</li>
                <li>Use <code>&lt;mark&gt;</code> to highlight relevant text.</li>
                <li>Use <code>&lt;time&gt;</code> to provide a machine-readable date.</li>
                <li>Use <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;summary&gt;</code> for expandable information.</li>
            </ol>
        </section>
        <section class="examples">
            <h2>A thought to remember</h2>
            <blockquote>
                <p>The web is more than what you see; it is also the meaning you give to it.</p>
                <footer>— A note for curious learners</footer>
            </blockquote>
            <p>
                Keep exploring with <cite>your favorite learning resource</cite>,
                and remember that semantic HTML helps browsers and people understand
                the content.
            </p>
            <p>
                Today’s practice session:
                <time datetime="2026-10-08">October 8, 2026</time>.
            </p>
            <details>
                <summary>Why use semantic HTML?</summary>
                <p>
                    Semantic elements describe the purpose of your content. That
                    makes pages easier to navigate, maintain, and understand with
                    assistive technologies.
                </p>
            </details>
        </section>
    </main>
    <footer>
        <p>&copy; 2026 Daily HTML Challenge</p>
    </footer>
</body>
</html>